WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:02.602
-You mentioned New York Governor
Andrew Cuomo --

00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:04.637 align:start size:94% position:6%
this is someone you've covered
for 20 years.

00:00:04.637 --> 00:00:06.940 size:88% position:44%
I think a lot of people
became introduced to him

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:11.077 align:start size:94% position:6%
based on his early
coronavirus press conferences,

00:00:11.077 --> 00:00:13.279 align:start size:94% position:6%
for which he was praised
for maybe being

00:00:13.279 --> 00:00:14.881 align:start size:94% position:6%
a little bit more
straight-talking

00:00:14.881 --> 00:00:15.548 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
than the president.

00:00:15.548 --> 00:00:18.018 size:78% position:39%
Are you surprised now
at the place he's in?

00:00:18.018 --> 00:00:21.121 align:start size:94% position:6%
Obviously,
we're hearing a lot about,

00:00:21.121 --> 00:00:23.256 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know,
him covering up the accoun

00:00:23.256 --> 00:00:25.558
of exactly how many people
passed away in nursing homes

00:00:25.558 --> 00:00:28.962 size:81% position:41%
as well as allegations
of sexual harassment.

00:00:28.962 --> 00:00:30.030 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, look,
I'm always surprised

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:34.567 align:start size:94% position:6%
when politicians behave
inappropriately or stupidly,

00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:36.036 line:92%
but, no, not particularly.

00:00:36.036 --> 00:00:40.106
I mean, anybody who's been
familiar with Andrew Cuomo

00:00:40.106 --> 00:00:42.242 align:start size:94% position:6%
is familiar
with the fact that --

00:00:42.242 --> 00:00:43.977 align:start size:94% position:6%
well, first of all,
when it comes to, you know

00:00:43.977 --> 00:00:47.847 align:start size:94% position:6%
accusations of him
being abrasive or bullying

00:00:47.847 --> 00:00:48.982 size:88% position:44% line:92%
that's just been his way

00:00:48.982 --> 00:00:51.151 align:start size:94% position:6%
the whole time
I've been aware of him.

00:00:51.151 --> 00:00:56.756 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, I remember in 2002
covering his gubernatorial run

00:00:56.756 --> 00:00:58.625 line:92%
he withdrew before he got --

00:00:58.625 --> 00:01:01.828 align:start size:94% position:6%
he would have been shellacke
in the Democratic primary

00:01:01.828 --> 00:01:02.929 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
by Carl McCall,

00:01:02.929 --> 00:01:04.230 align:start size:94% position:6%
who went on to get
the nomination

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:06.132 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then lose to
Governor Pataki.

00:01:06.132 --> 00:01:10.236
And I don't know that he -
remember, he was a HUD --

00:01:10.236 --> 00:01:11.071 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
he was Secretary

00:01:11.071 --> 00:01:13.339 align:start size:94% position:6%
of the Department of Housing
and Urban Development

00:01:13.339 --> 00:01:14.607 align:start size:94% position:6%
during the Clinton
administration.

00:01:14.607 --> 00:01:18.111
I'm not sure that anybody else
from the Clinton administratio

00:01:18.111 --> 00:01:18.645 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
endorsed him,

00:01:18.645 --> 00:01:22.215 align:start size:94% position:6%
even though he had worked
in the Clinton administration.

00:01:22.215 --> 00:01:23.349 line:92%
And, look, he has a long -

00:01:23.349 --> 00:01:26.586 size:75% position:38%
has had a reputation
for being bullying,

00:01:26.586 --> 00:01:29.355
and so, no, this isn't -- this
isn't particularly surprising.

00:01:29.355 --> 00:01:33.159
I am surprised by the covering
up of the nursing-home data,

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:37.597
because, as always, it's not
the crime, it's the cover-up

00:01:37.597 --> 00:01:38.398 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
What -- you know,

00:01:38.398 --> 00:01:41.234
what he did with
the nursing home is certainly --

00:01:41.234 --> 00:01:43.736 align:start size:94% position:6%
nursing homes,
forcing them to take in people

00:01:43.736 --> 00:01:46.806 align:start size:94% position:6%
even if they have COVID,
you know,

00:01:46.806 --> 00:01:48.708 align:start size:94% position:6%
was a very questionable
decision.

00:01:48.708 --> 00:01:50.743 line:92%
But then covering up the data --

00:01:50.743 --> 00:01:54.981
that's really the kind of thin
that gets somebody in trouble.

00:01:54.981 --> 00:01:57.884 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And based on the fact,
you know, now he's said that

00:01:57.884 --> 00:02:01.588 align:start size:94% position:6%
he unintentionally
made women feel uncomfortable,

00:02:01.588 --> 00:02:02.956 line:92%
he's apologized for that -

00:02:02.956 --> 00:02:07.127 align:start size:94% position:6%
it seems like he is trying
to move on from it.

00:02:07.127 --> 00:02:09.229 line:92%
Do you think this is something

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:13.600 align:start size:94% position:6%
that he gets through
and survives politically?

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:14.868 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, I mean,
he seems to be doing

00:02:14.868 --> 00:02:18.338 align:start size:94% position:6%
the Governor Ralph Northam
approach --

00:02:18.338 --> 00:02:20.240 line:92%
remember him from Virginia -

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:22.675 align:start size:94% position:6%
he had the blackface
controversy.

00:02:22.675 --> 00:02:25.545 align:start size:94% position:6%
And he ignored all the calls
for his resignation,

00:02:25.545 --> 00:02:27.347 line:92%
and he basically survived.

00:02:27.347 --> 00:02:28.915 align:start size:94% position:6%
Maybe it's
the Donald Trump approach.

00:02:28.915 --> 00:02:31.384 align:start size:94% position:6%
People thought he should
withdraw from the --

00:02:31.384 --> 00:02:31.951 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
when he was --

00:02:31.951 --> 00:02:34.020 align:start size:94% position:6%
after the "Access Hollywood"
video came out,

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:34.888 line:92%
people said he should withdraw

00:02:34.888 --> 00:02:36.823 align:start size:94% position:6%
and he ended up
being elected president.

00:02:36.823 --> 00:02:39.192 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, I don't know.
I mean, I guess it really --

00:02:39.192 --> 00:02:40.026 line:92%
having talked to some members,

00:02:40.026 --> 00:02:41.794 align:start size:94% position:6%
some Democratic members
of Congress

00:02:41.794 --> 00:02:42.595 size:84% position:42% line:92%
who are critical of him

00:02:42.595 --> 00:02:45.598 align:start size:94% position:6%
but have not called
for his resignation,

00:02:45.598 --> 00:02:46.866 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think
it really depends on what

00:02:46.866 --> 00:02:49.068 align:start size:94% position:6%
the New York Attorney Genera
Letitia James

00:02:49.068 --> 00:02:51.337 line:92%
finds in her investigation

00:02:51.337 --> 00:02:56.709
Because, if there is more,
or if she comes out and says

00:02:56.709 --> 00:02:59.979
"Governor Cuomo sexually
harassed these two women,"

00:02:59.979 --> 00:03:02.315 align:start size:94% position:6%
then, that's gonna be toug
for him,

00:03:02.315 --> 00:03:03.183 size:81% position:41% line:92%
but we'll have to see.

00:03:03.183 --> 00:03:04.250 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think a lot of people
are punting

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:07.253 align:start size:94% position:6%
and waiting to see what
that investigation entails

00:03:07.253 --> 00:03:08.454 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Last thing
I want to ask you about --

00:03:08.454 --> 00:03:11.357 align:start size:94% position:6%
you wrote a political thriller
called "The Hellfire Club,

00:03:11.357 --> 00:03:14.260
and now you have a sequel coming
out in a couple of months.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:15.828 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I do.
-Has it been nice

00:03:15.828 --> 00:03:17.697 line:92%
to have something fictional,

00:03:17.697 --> 00:03:21.668 align:start size:94% position:6%
albeit one that is inspire
by historical events,

00:03:21.668 --> 00:03:25.605
to sort of find solace in
during these trying times?

00:03:25.605 --> 00:03:29.475
-Yeah. I wrote a lot of it
during the pandemic last yea

00:03:29.475 --> 00:03:30.376 line:92%
when I didn't have a commute

00:03:30.376 --> 00:03:33.813 align:start size:94% position:6%
because I was broadcasting
from my house.

00:03:33.813 --> 00:03:35.682 line:92%
So, from April to July, I mean

00:03:35.682 --> 00:03:38.151 align:start size:94% position:6%
I just didn't have two hours
in a car every day,

00:03:38.151 --> 00:03:40.253 line:92%
then I suddenly had some time.

00:03:40.253 --> 00:03:43.856 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, yeah,
it takes place in 1962.

00:03:43.856 --> 00:03:45.592 align:start size:94% position:6%
The main characters
from the first book,

00:03:45.592 --> 00:03:46.292 size:78% position:39% line:92%
Charlie and Margaret,

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:48.428 align:start size:94% position:6%
go and investigate
Sinatra and the Rat Pack.

00:03:48.428 --> 00:03:51.531 align:start size:94% position:6%
They're blackmailed
by Attorney General Kennedy.

00:03:51.531 --> 00:03:52.632 line:92%
So, yeah, that was a lot of fu

00:03:52.632 --> 00:03:56.869 align:start size:94% position:6%
to just, like, plunge into
1962 Hollywood in Los Angeles,

00:03:56.869 --> 00:04:01.541
in that whole world of Hitchcock
and John Frankenheimer

00:04:01.541 --> 00:04:03.843 align:start size:94% position:6%
and Marilyn Monroe,
and that was --

00:04:03.843 --> 00:04:05.078 line:92%
yes, to not pay attention

00:04:05.078 --> 00:04:07.847 align:start size:94% position:6%
to the dystopia that
we lived through last summer -

00:04:07.847 --> 00:04:10.149 align:start size:94% position:6%
that was --
that was miserable --

00:04:10.149 --> 00:04:11.317 line:92%
kids being schooled at home,

00:04:11.317 --> 00:04:14.354 align:start size:94% position:6%
angry -- angry friends
and family members,

00:04:14.354 --> 00:04:17.323 align:start size:94% position:6%
and President Trump doing
everything he did --

00:04:17.323 --> 00:04:18.324 align:start size:94% position:6%
we don't have to
go back into that.

00:04:18.324 --> 00:04:21.261
Yeah, it was nice to spend
some time with the Rat Pack.

00:04:21.261 --> 00:04:23.896 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, hopefully, we'll ge
you back in a couple of months

00:04:23.896 --> 00:04:25.064 align:start size:94% position:6%
to talk about your book
when it comes out.

00:04:25.064 --> 00:04:27.000 align:start size:94% position:6%
Thanks so much for making time
for us today, Jake.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.003 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Thanks, Seth.
Love to Lexi.

